Package: timecrate
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         gnupg,
         gpg-agent,
         openssl,
         python3:any,
         rclone,
         zstd
Recommends: qrencode
Suggests: timecrate-gui
Description: encrypted, signed off-site disaster-recovery backup
 Timecrate ships the irreplaceable slice of a machine - configuration,
 secrets, identity, hand-tuned state - to a cloud remote as a single
 compressed, encrypted and signed archive. Bulk that can be reinstalled or
 re-downloaded is deliberately left out; a manifest of installed packages,
 enabled units and desktop settings rides along instead and is replayed at
 restore time.
 .
 Every kit is signed as well as encrypted, and every path that extracts one
 verifies that signature before writing a byte - so an attacker who gains write
 access to the cloud remote cannot plant an archive that a restore would unpack
 as root. Retention is grandfather-father-son, kits can be mirrored to a second
 provider, and restoring an archive older than the newest recorded one is
 refused unless you ask for it explicitly.
 .
 Recovery deliberately needs nothing but gpg, zstd and tar, so a restore does
 not depend on this package being installable. A scheduled drill proves that
 claim against a real archive using only the escrowed key. The backup, drill
 and capstone timers are enabled on installation and kept across upgrades.

Package: timecrate-gui
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         timecrate (= ${binary:Version}),
         gir1.2-adw-1,
         gir1.2-gtk-4.0,
         pkexec,
         python3:any,
         python3-gi
Description: encrypted, signed off-site disaster-recovery backup (graphical interface)
 A GTK4 and libadwaita front-end for Timecrate: run and watch a backup,
 browse the archives held on the remote and restore one, run a recovery drill,
 and edit the settings that the command-line tool reads.
 .
 This package is separate so that a headless machine can run backups without
 pulling in a desktop stack.
